INT. OPERA HOUSE INFIRMARY - EVENING

The stalls have been torn out and replaced with rows of iron cots. Gilt cherubs look down over bandaged men. Through a blown-out window, a wintry sky the color of ash.

SERGEANT BRONISLAW KRAJEWSKI (40s, thick moustache, arm in a sling) sits on the edge of a cot, mending a boot by lantern light. LIEUTENANT SIPHO MBEKI (30s, upright, clean spectacles) writes a letter on a drum case. NURSE FIONNUALA DOYLE (30s, red hair under a veil, sleeves rolled) kneels to change a dressing.

A small figure sits cross-legged on the stage, back to them, a boy in a coat too big, watching the dust fall through the light. Nobody has asked him a thing.

KRAJEWSKI
That one has not spoken since the bridge.

MBEKI
Perhaps he has nothing to say.

DOYLE
He ate. That is something.

THE BOY
(without turning)
It was mine.

They all stop. The boy does not move. Doyle stands.

DOYLE
What was yours, love?

THE BOY
The bread. You gave it to the other man. I only wanted to know why.

Krajewski lowers the boot. Mbeki sets down his pen.

MBEKI
What is your name, young man?

THE BOY
Ilario.

MBEKI
Ilario. A good name. Mine is Sipho.

ILARIO
(turning, finally)
What does it mean?

MBEKI
Gift.

Ilario studies him. His eyes are very old.

ILARIO
Then why does everyone here look as if they've lost one?

Doyle sits on the stage beside him. Krajewski clears his throat, puts down the boot, and stands, one hand on his sling.

KRAJEWSKI
Ilario. Come, eat with us.

Ilario hesitates. Then he rises and walks to the cot, small and dignified. Krajewski tears the last of his own bread and hands him half. Mbeki sets aside the letter and pours three cups of water.
